def c(n,l,p,a,g):t.card(s(n),l,p,a,g)
c(1,'01','Explain why an invented goods trade deficit widens when exports rise N$80m to N$100m and imports N$90m to N$120m.','Export receipts rise N$20m but import payments rise N$30m. Goods trade therefore moves from −N$10m to −N$20m; export growth alone does not establish an improved trade balance.',['Starting change (1): both flows rise. Because/mechanism (2): export increase N$20m (1), imports N$30m (1). Consequence (1): deficit widens N$10m to N$20m.'])
c(2,'01','Explain why a higher value of imported machinery need not mean greater current consumer spending on foreign goods.','Machinery is productive equipment rather than household consumption. The composition of imports changes the interpretation even though both contribute to import payments.',['Context (1): imported equipment. Reason/conclusion (1): distinguish investment-related goods from consumer imports.'])
c(11,'01','Explain the error in treating a goods trade deficit as the whole balance of payments.','Goods trade compares goods exports and imports. The balance of payments also includes services, income, transfers and financing, so the narrower balance cannot stand for the full account.',['Confusion (1): goods subset treated as full account. Boundary/test (1): identify other external components.'])
c(13,'01','Explain why an overseas investor’s acquisition of a lasting ownership interest in a domestic workshop is not itself a current-account goods export.','The ownership investment is a financial-account flow. A later sale of workshop output abroad would be a separate export of current goods.',['Confusion (1): investment funding mistaken for export. Boundary/test (1): ownership transaction versus sale of output.'])
c(3,'02','Explain how stronger foreign demand for a currency can cause appreciation in a hypothetical floating economy, with currency supply and other relevant conditions unchanged.','Foreign buyers need the currency for purchases or investment. Demand increases against unchanged supply, raising its price in other currencies. This hypothetical mechanism does not describe an independent Namibia dollar/rand movement under the maintained peg.',['Starting change (1): currency demand rises. Mechanism (2): purchases require currency (1), unchanged supply meets stronger demand (1). Consequence (1): appreciation under floating conditions.'])
c(4,'02','Explain the import-cost effect of US$1 rising from N$18 to N$20 for a US$100 input, with the rand peg maintained and its dollar price unchanged.','The Namibia dollar depreciates against the US dollar. The input rises from N$1,800 to N$2,000, a N$200 increase; there is no independent movement against the rand in this case.',['Context (1): third-currency quotation and fixed dollar price. Reason/consequence (1): N$200 dearer input under maintained peg.'])
table='Invented equal-resource one-period maxima: A 12 mats or 6 baskets; B 4 mats or 4 baskets. Constant opportunity costs, comparable goods, no transport costs.'
c(5,'03',table+' Calculate the opportunity cost of one basket for each producer.','A sacrifices 12/6 = 2 mats per basket; B sacrifices 4/4 = 1 mat per basket.',['Formula/substitution (1): alternative mats divided by baskets. Result/units (1): A 2 and B 1 mat per basket.'])
c(6,'03',table+' Analyse the absolute and comparative advantages.','A has absolute advantage in both goods. A’s mat costs half a basket versus B’s one, so A has comparative advantage in mats; B’s basket costs one mat versus A’s two, so B has comparative advantage in baskets.',['Pattern (1): A greater absolute output both. Evidence (1): 12/6 versus 4/4. Explanation (2): A lower mat cost (1), B lower basket cost (1).'])
c(12,'03',table+' Explain the error in concluding that B cannot gain from trade because A has absolute advantage in both goods.','B has the lower opportunity cost of baskets, one mat rather than two. Trade between those opportunity costs can benefit both, despite A’s absolute output advantage.',['Confusion (1): absolute advantage substituted for comparative. Boundary/test (1): B lower basket opportunity cost permits mutual gain.'])
c(7,'03-M01','Define absolute advantage.','Producing more with the same resources, or the same output with fewer resources.',['Definition/key term (1): resource-productivity advantage.'])
c(8,'03-M01',table+' Identify the absolute-advantage producer for each good.','A for mats and A for baskets, using the same resource comparison.',['Feature/example (1): mats A. Distinction/characteristic (1): baskets A.'])
c(9,'03-M02','Define comparative advantage.','Producing at a lower opportunity cost.',['Definition/key term (1): lower alternative-output sacrifice.'])
c(10,'03-M02','Identify the comparative basket producer from supplied costs: A sacrifices two mats per basket; B sacrifices one. Include the deciding comparison.','B, because one mat is a lower sacrifice than two mats per basket.',['Feature (1): B. Distinction/example (1): lower opportunity cost, 1 versus 2 mats.'])
