t.card(t.wo['performance_tasks'][1],'01 02 03',data+' Describe three features: goods balance change, currency movement and comparative basket advantage (6). Explain the comparative-advantage basis for specialisation and the limitation of export growth alone as evidence of external improvement (7). Discuss whether the third-currency movement benefits both illustrative businesses equally (7).','Goods deficit widens N$10m to N$20m; the Namibia dollar depreciates against the dollar under the maintained rand peg; B has basket advantage. A mat costs half a basket versus one for B, while B basket costs one mat versus two. Trade terms between the costs can support mutual gain, but imports grew faster than exports in the separate account. Exporter may benefit with responsive buyers and spare capacity; importer faces dearer essential inputs and limited substitution. Benefits are unequal and trade improvement is conditional.',['Data description (6): deficit widens (1), 10 to 20 detail (1); depreciation (1), N$18 to N$20 third-currency detail (1); B basket advantage (1), 1 versus 2 mat cost (1). Mechanism/explanation (7): A mat cost .5 basket (1), B mat cost 1 (1), B basket cost 1 mat (1), A basket cost 2 (1), between-cost trade permits gain (1); import increase exceeds exports (1), full external account wider than goods (1). Both sides/judgement (7): exporter receipt/price opportunity (1), spare capacity supports response (1); importer input cost rises (1), limited substitution (1); condition buyer response/contracts and peg (1); judgement unequal effects (1), no guaranteed aggregate trade gain (1). Marks allocation 6+7+7=20.'],[data])
